know i i became actually i got a  little bit upset  because uh i said hey i'm bringing more  money in than you guys have ever seen  before and i'm being held  accountable like this  and i started to get a little angry and  then i realized i'm talking to the boss  you don't get angry with the boss you  know i said i'm going to walk out of  here i'd be better keep my composure but  i was mad you know after the fear when i  got into the room then i got angry but  kept my cool as usual  and walked out of the room but it was it  was an impression that was made on me  and i i didn't like it you know i said  this is not what this should be about  um  because i was loyal to the family i mean  i was doing the right thing and uh you  know you don't want to be held  accountable when you're doing the right  thing  what was the point that got you in  prison  well like i said i became a major target  of law enforcement i was indicted five  times in the state  and i went to trial five times and i was  either acquitted or dismissed in every  case i beat them all  and then i was indicted in the state of  florida on a racketeering case i was  dated in  new york on two federal racketeering  cases one brought on by rudy giuliani  i'm sure you know that name  and i went to trial on that one for  several months and i was acquitted in  that case also so i had a pretty good  record of beating them at trial  but i finally took a plea to this whole  another racketeering case it involved  tax fraud  mainly the gasoline case so i took a  plea on that because  i knew eventually i was going to go down  i mean i was too big a name i was too  big a target  and  unfortunately in new york when these  racketeering laws came in if you got  convicted on racketeering there was guys  that were getting 50 and 100 years  and i was one of the youngest guys i  would have probably gotten 200 years if  they convicted me so i realized  i was in trouble so i tried to cut my  losses  i had met a young woman that i felt very  much in love with she's now my wife of  37 years  and she was really the catalyst that  caused me to  have second thoughts about remaining in  that life and trying to get out so when  i took a plea i got a 10 year prison  sentence i had a 15 million dollar  restitution i had a jet plane and a  helicopter i had to surrender it all  as part of my forfeiture  and  i thought that was the best way to cut  my losses you know do some time in  prison give the government some money  marry this new young girl move out to  california and try to get away from the  life so that was kind of the plan  i personally lost  between legal fees uh you know money  that i surrendered to the government my  you know  close to 100 million dollars i mean uh  listen i tell everybody all the time you  know you get involved with the  government  believe me even if you win the case it's  going to cost you so much money in lost  revenue and money to legal fees to  defend yourself if you do happen to go  to prison the losses that you take you  know through that time i mean it cost me  a lot of money there's no question about  it so  the the fbi and federal government they  tried to make a cooperating witness out  of me they really wanted me to cooperate  when i walked away from the life you  know there was a contract on my life  from my boss because you're not allowed  to do that and people are upset with me  on the street my father even was was  upset with me so the government used  that they came into the prison they said  you know you're a dead man anyway  francis cooperate with us we'll put you  in the program witness protection  program but i want to do that i wasn't  looking to hurt anybody wasn't mad  anybody i just wanted out of their life  they didn't take no for an answer so  they kind of moved me around to  different prisons you know never letting  me get too comfortable  and i spent two and a half years  actually 29 months and seven days and  solitary i was in the hole  and that's a six by eight cell 24 7. and  they kept me in solitary lockdown for  quite some time so  they gave me a rough time  what did it feel like to be in solitary  confinement for such a long time  solitary is difficult uh you know i  learned through that experience we  weren't meant to be solo creatures we  were meant to be social  and a lot of guys when the lights went  out at night you heard a lot of moaning  and groaning a lot of guys did not do  well and i understood it  for me it was it was difficult no doubt  um  but i got through it you know i became a  person of faith uh you know through that  experience  and that helped me tremendously i had a  very strong determination to get home to  my wife and my little babies that were  at home  and i fought my way out of there you  know it was uh  it was tough though i don't want to let  anybody think anything different  solitary is is a very difficult  situation to navigate  so talking about your life after prison  how did you adjust to it  you know  it was a hard adjustment because i had  to leave new york i moved out to  california because i would have never  made it in new york  you walk away from that life and you  stay in new york i wouldn't blast it no  doubt  but i had to rebuild myself under some  difficult conditions i still had people  on the street upset with me i knew  people were trying to come after me so i  had to be mindful of that  and then i was like a fish out of water  for a while you know i mean i never  really had a job  the last job i had was when i was 16  years old you know working in high  school  in the summer so i always work for  myself i always had to get things going  on my own so  i have to come out in la and now start a  whole new life so it was difficult  um  you know but again i'm a pretty  determined guy i'm fairly resourceful  and uh have a head for business so we  got started it took while you know it  took took a couple of years before i  really got on my feet and uh again you  know i was fortunate and that the the  whole speaking career that started for  me was nothing i ever envisioned  but it worked out you know from the  moment i got out of prison i was sought  after to to speak with certain  organizations so i do a lot of work with  juvenile delinquents gangbangers i spent  a lot of time with prison with a lot of  kids coming into the system at a young  age  damn everybody looks at mafia cozanostra  as the biggest gang in the world and you  know to some degree we were so when i  speak to them i speak from experience i  have credibility and many of them listen  they really do and i think we were able  to save a lot of lives or redirect a lot  of lives you know as a result of of that  you know i tell them it's important who  you're accountable to in life when i was  on the street i was accountable to my  oath i was accountable to my boss as a  result i spent every day in criminal  activity you know and i ended up in  prison we as adults  created the environment that these young  people are growing up in today and it's  tough man it's tough to navigate and we  got to help them so i try to do my part  when you look back at everything you've  been through  how do you feel about your life  well you know look people ask me do i  have regrets and obviously there's  things that i did in my former life that  uh i regret now  you know there's times i think i deal  with that my wife says to me you know  there's a lot of times i'm fighting in  my sleep you know and things come back  to you because you know you see a lot  during that time it's like you know the  way soldiers who experience things in  the war when things are horrific you  know you remember them  and and you can't make up for what  you've done in the past it's done you  know i i don't do what i do to make up  for that i do what i do because as a  person of faith i believe that you know  god knows our hearts he knows our  intents we can't pull a scam on him we  can't defraud god so if we're doing the  right things um  you know it'll work out in the end you  know the the the horrible thing is i  speak to so many men when i go into  prisons i have a prison ministry they're  in their 50s and 60s and they look back  and they say mike look what i've done  with my life i spent 15 years 20 years  here i lost my wife my kids don't talk  to me and i tell them straight out the  street life is dead end you can end up  in prison or maybe dead at some point in  time i tell them straight out also in  this life we are who we hang out with  you pick the wrong crowd you hang with  the wrong crowd they're going to lead  you in the wrong direction you're going  to get in trouble  tomorrow  i'll sit at seven o'clock in the morning  go and get kennedy that was my sign-off  shotgun it was named after president  kennedy i said i want one chamber one  buck shot filled with rock salt  and the other one with the normal bug  shot and all right yeah i'm not messing  about with these people they're  dangerous i'm going to shoot
 

